{"title":"2000 AMC 10 Problems/Problem 20","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Lagrange_Multipliers_/_Calculus)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Lagrange Multipliers / Calculus)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Daily_Dose_of_Math\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Daily Dose of Math</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_10_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be nonnegative integers such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/1/42196d691c1ae7a78b9fae90282c447441d3209d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A+M+C=10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"133\" height=\"14\" />.  What is the maximum value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/f/e3facdda5fdd764469c4908c0d16e61bb359b1c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A\\cdot M\\cdot C+A\\cdot M+M\\cdot C+C\\cdot A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"275\" height=\"14\" />?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/0/3c025f075fe6e11c81e5241c94c460988d0eed2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 49 \\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ 59 \\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ 69 \\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ 79 \\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ 89$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"394\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_10_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The trick is to realize that the sum <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/7/bb79f9bcb791511c47b8ea2d41d5e59d2f11d9d1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AMC+AM+MC+CA$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"209\" height=\"14\" /> is similar to the product <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/c/bcc8f945bccd2c8ea01c84edf2161a3a309efbd3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(A+1)(M+1)(C+1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"182\" height=\"18\" />. If we multiply <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/c/bcc8f945bccd2c8ea01c84edf2161a3a309efbd3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(A+1)(M+1)(C+1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"182\" height=\"18\" />, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/9/e79c4c48392c38962ab3145486a05378da98ecee.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(A+1)(M+1)(C+1) = AMC + AM + AC + MC + A + M + C + 1.\\]\" width=\"484\" height=\"18\" /> We know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/1/42196d691c1ae7a78b9fae90282c447441d3209d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A+M+C=10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"133\" height=\"14\" />, therefore <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/a/9ea1697726ca6fb2a57b0b140374bcce179361f5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(A+1)(M+1)(C+1) = (AMC + AM + MC + CA) + 11$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"471\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/6/fe6654be876b64240487233093be11f4973c3301.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[AMC + AM + MC + CA = (A+1)(M+1)(C+1) - 11.\\]\" width=\"460\" height=\"18\" /> Now consider the maximal value of this expression. Suppose that some two of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> differ by at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.  Then this triple <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/0/d90ffab29406216fed4300bd976083c6779df3cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(A,M,C)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"75\" height=\"18\" /> is not optimal. (To see this, WLOG let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/6/8260d2caa4e1d3bc8ffef4a7324766d88f1982ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A\\geq C+2.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"87\" height=\"15\" /> We can then increase the value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/c/bcc8f945bccd2c8ea01c84edf2161a3a309efbd3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(A+1)(M+1)(C+1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"182\" height=\"18\" /> by changing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/f/b7f701b49d66c94428ba820e498326be6f7adb58.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A \\to A-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"86\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/f/90fcb69a2946c156ef4541f5788355eb27cfbc40.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C \\to C+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"87\" height=\"14\" />. Or, we can consider the equality case of the AM-GM inequality, which would maximize this product; intuitively, the closest we can get to that equality case is what we have below.)\n</p><p>Therefore the maximum is achieved when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/0/d90ffab29406216fed4300bd976083c6779df3cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(A,M,C)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"75\" height=\"18\" /> is a permutation of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/4/574bc3188039b9fc96c4d32b0a42fdf37400edee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(3,3,4)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"18\" />. The value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/c/bcc8f945bccd2c8ea01c84edf2161a3a309efbd3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(A+1)(M+1)(C+1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"182\" height=\"18\" /> in this case is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/2/cc2871db637dfc33c23bfaeb0281ba1f17c8b433.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4\\cdot 4\\cdot 5=80,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"100\" height=\"16\" /> and thus the maximum of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/5/4f578dc012b13b972cc5023f080df0b79150caaa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AMC + AM + MC + CA$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"209\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/d/21d7a58cf402efb238384c1063cdd9acaa545229.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$80-11 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C)}\\ 69}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"150\" height=\"28\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_10_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Notice that if we want to maximize <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/9/5a91fa59935c0093911a7ad6a3e1c5fe93a8dc7e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AMC + AM + MC + AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"209\" height=\"14\" />, we want A, M, and C to be as close as possible. For example, if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/3/5238d15917eb232a4af35888007425d1fd8cfb35.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A = 7, B = 2,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"107\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/4/2f454b6392cf3734eef64bbd307daf4ffa3c0bca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C=1,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"51\" height=\"16\" /> then the expression would have a much smaller value than if we were to substitute <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/1/9211042cf8eb2d924883812949087554cbbcd91c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A = 4, B = 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"102\" height=\"16\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/e/77e3435fdaadad9195048f672956b8e6bef8db23.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"46\" height=\"13\" />. So to make A, B, and C as close together as possible, we divide <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/f/54fdd6f7231698f4ab7ea416edf530c48e841d9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{10}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"20\" height=\"37\" /> to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Therefore, A must be 3, M must be 3, and C must be 4.  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/d/d3dc31b91cb9ed3e2751468a17d87ab6ebe5e5b8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AMC + AM + MC + AC = 36 + 12 + 12 + 9 = 69$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"405\" height=\"14\" />. So the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/6/e261485fab9bdfe76dd5257db762f800e084c441.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C)}\\ 69.}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"69\" height=\"28\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_10_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>According to our knowledge in AM-GM, the closer numbers are, the value of their product is bigger. Assume that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/4/cd4279d75b55beeefe351ce528f36a27f04fcac7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A=B&lt;C$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"91\" height=\"13\" />, we can get the set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/3/ba36c39a6775c9514a6194b076b0d81b4d9b2050.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A=3,B=3,C=4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"161\" height=\"18\" /> which the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/6/e261485fab9bdfe76dd5257db762f800e084c441.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C)}\\ 69.}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"69\" height=\"28\" />\n~bluesoul\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Lagrange_Multipliers_.2F_Calculus.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Lagrange_Multipliers_/_Calculus)\">Solution 4 (Lagrange Multipliers / Calculus)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_10_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Lagrange Multipliers / Calculus)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>This problem can be modeled as an optimization problem and solved using multivariable calculus.\n</p><p>Our objective function is defined as:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/a/77ad6051a2f9a5c45bba8e72284e2ecfe27fdee0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f(A, M, C) = AMC + AM + MC + AC\\]\" width=\"320\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Subject to the constraint:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/5/33547ccbc7a23f8d2398546f5ec65f99fcca2fc1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[g(A, M, C) = A + M + C - 10 = 0\\]\" width=\"275\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>We set up the Lagrangian function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/7/1c7c4018118f2030ca7c55caa98a95b5a59aea7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{L}(A, M, C, \\lambda)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"109\" height=\"20\" /> by subtracting the product of the multiplier <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/4/ce4588fd900d02afcbd260bc07f54cce49a7dc4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lambda$\" width=\"10\" height=\"13\" /> and the constraint function from the objective function:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/0/6f03c43dbe7e66077cf02bdc6a24867d19b5314e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\mathcal{L}(A, M, C, \\lambda) = f(A, M, C) - \\lambda \\cdot g(A, M, C)\\]\" width=\"349\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/2/af25d8e1863a8b712adfa50623643ade9cd0ce7a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\mathcal{L}(A, M, C, \\lambda) = AMC + AM + MC + AC - \\lambda(A + M + C - 10)\\]\" width=\"517\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/3/423f18d59e986f813952e880d59daa3ddd654944.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\mathcal{L}(A, M, C, \\lambda) = AMC + AM + MC + AC - \\lambda A - \\lambda M - \\lambda C + 10\\lambda\\]\" width=\"536\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>The critical points of this system are found by setting the gradient <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c7adb376de99a84d826115f4c5ef7fa3b592bb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\nabla\\mathcal{L}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> equal to the zero vector. To compute the partial derivatives, we differentiate with respect to one variable while treating all other variables as constants. Setting each partial derivative to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> gives us the following system of equations:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/5/bd5cc0c84307619bf30d82e0070e4bd075a2687a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial A} = MC + M + C - \\lambda = 0 \\implies \\lambda = MC + M + C \\quad \\text{(Eq. 1)}\\]\" width=\"494\" height=\"38\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/0/1f04177b87491b8a3449d2aa7ea4da0847034606.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial M} = AC + A + C - \\lambda = 0 \\implies \\lambda = AC + A + C \\quad \\text{(Eq. 2)}\\]\" width=\"476\" height=\"38\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/5/73536eade314077eebb13fe024c3d12ce68ab608.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial C} = AM + A + M - \\lambda = 0 \\implies \\lambda = AM + A + M \\quad \\text{(Eq. 3)}\\]\" width=\"492\" height=\"38\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/6/a76473a2071309dd5bcbb7b592514235f1759d27.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial \\lambda} = -(A + M + C - 10) = 0 \\implies A + M + C = 10 \\quad \\text{(Eq. 4)}\\]\" width=\"496\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>By equating <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/9/ed9c6a79aa1749d943c542967c8e067cdc7f279c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{Eq. 1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"42\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/6/b262d56d2eda6738259da61f9857c3c8d916062b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{Eq. 2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"42\" height=\"17\" />, we get:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/7/b87ec724c9e2ff0148ae6564f322606d213e1165.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[MC + M + C = AC + A + C\\]\" width=\"236\" height=\"14\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/8/608f012079d95998dd78b5d65fd4ca5dede4a364.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[MC + M = AC + A\\]\" width=\"163\" height=\"14\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/d/03d434ad8e170aaeb0d281f63d6f1775eba3c745.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[MC + M - AC - A = 0\\]\" width=\"194\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>Grouping terms and factoring gives:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/c/8cc8b3b4a8399152fc4bd8a192ec2e61870c2f46.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(M - A)(C + 1) = 0\\]\" width=\"160\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>This equation holds if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/d/a2d62bf83ee5e10b285a68218260202fd3505f3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M = A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"59\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/6/006ee9ae9ac90fc5ddc6d73960296f9b0a6ec13b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C = -1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"60\" height=\"13\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/4/c345e12575913b55d7a7baa1014cde39be65c5ba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A, M,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"44\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> are given to be non-negative integers, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> cannot be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/e/bae5aba07d37ff6ff813107e76260fb31ad5794e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"25\" height=\"15\" />, meaning only the former condition (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/d/a2d62bf83ee5e10b285a68218260202fd3505f3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M = A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"59\" height=\"14\" />) can hold.\n</p><p>By symmetry, equating <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/6/b262d56d2eda6738259da61f9857c3c8d916062b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{Eq. 2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"42\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/e/f7e2d4ec4983b619b8b6e30fee8665dcc3cdd515.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{Eq. 3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"42\" height=\"17\" /> yields <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/4/894a348ddbb840d820050384d3706153bd2255a8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A = C$\" width=\"52\" height=\"13\" />. Thus, the critical point occurs when all three variables are equal:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/8/ab8fcb28395456718f56df6813ea53213fd61392.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[A = M = C\\]\" width=\"95\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p>Substituting this into the original constraint (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/9/92939fe8b526e27de78fa634ce125505fada91dc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{Eq. 4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"42\" height=\"17\" />):\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/d/f9d032a58dbafa6c4e35476f687f2e0145effebd.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[3A = 10 \\implies A = M = C = \\frac{10}{3}\\]\" width=\"255\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>In the real domain, the function reaches its absolute maximum at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/1/bf1a42fe16c9372f664b1659112235bf51f3383e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(\\frac{10}{3}, \\frac{10}{3}, \\frac{10}{3}\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"111\" height=\"45\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/4/c345e12575913b55d7a7baa1014cde39be65c5ba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A, M,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"44\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> must be integers, we look for the closest points of integral coordinates that satisfy the constraint. This is achieved by distributing the values as evenly as possible: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/9/7194b859269e3c110f4edc4664d43458011dffe1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3, 3,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"33\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> (in any order).\n</p><p>Plugging these integer values into our objective function:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/f/1afcf3975480dcb99c38264ef2d693450dc0b5f8.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f(3, 3, 4) = (3)(3)(4) + (3)(3) + (3)(4) + (3)(4) = 36 + 9 + 12 + 12 = 69\\]\" width=\"539\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Thus, the maximum value is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/6/e261485fab9bdfe76dd5257db762f800e084c441.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C)}\\ 69.}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"69\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p><p>~Fernat123\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_10_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Vdou0LpTlzY&amp;t=22s\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Vdou0LpTlzY&amp;t=22s</a>\n</p><p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ECzZPuuQMbE\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ECzZPuuQMbE</a>    ~David\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Daily_Dose_of_Math\">Video Solution by Daily Dose of Math</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_10_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Daily Dose of Math\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/xXBepDeeHAQ\">https://youtu.be/xXBepDeeHAQ</a>\n</p><p>~Thesmartgreekmathdude\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_10_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_10\" title=\"2000 AMC 10\">2000 AMC 10</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_10_Problems\" title=\"2000 AMC 10 Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_10_Answer_Key\" title=\"2000 AMC 10 Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_10_Problems/Problem_19\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2000 AMC 10 Problems/Problem 19\">Problem 19</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_10_Problems/Problem_21\" title=\"2000 AMC 10 Problems/Problem 21\">Problem 21</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_10_Problems/Problem_1\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2000 AMC 10 Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_10_Problems/Problem_2\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2000 AMC 10 Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_10_Problems/Problem_3\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2000 AMC 10 Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_10_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2000 AMC 10 Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_10_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2000 AMC 10 Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/i